1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,222
What useful work should a Bittensor subnet reward?

2
00:00:03,372 --> 00:00:06,042
That question is where a project begins.

3
00:00:06,192 --> 00:00:13,291
Turning the answer into software usually means moving between an editor, a terminal, evaluation scripts and chain tools.

4
00:00:13,441 --> 00:00:22,555
TensorStudio brings those pieces into a browser workspace, giving builders a practical path from an idea to code they can inspect and improve.

5
00:00:25,150 --> 00:00:29,576
Start in TensorScan, choose testnet, and watch the observed blocks advance.

6
00:00:29,726 --> 00:00:37,065
Each block arrives from the real Bittensor network, with finality and decoded activity visible in the same view.

7
00:00:37,215 --> 00:00:41,641
This gives our work a concrete reference point: current chain activity.

8
00:00:41,791 --> 00:00:50,793
Public chain reads are available independently of a miner process, so we can explore Bittensor before setting up our own subnet infrastructure.

9
00:00:55,150 --> 00:00:57,839
Now bring an idea into the workspace.

10
00:00:57,989 --> 00:01:03,924
Ask for a simple task tracker, review a suggested direction, and continue into the editor.

11
00:01:04,074 --> 00:01:07,980
The conversation, editable project files and app preview sit together.

12
00:01:08,130 --> 00:01:12,847
Open a file to understand the implementation, then try the resulting interface.

13
00:01:12,997 --> 00:01:19,743
The useful loop is straightforward: describe the behavior, inspect the code, run it, and make a change.

14
00:01:19,893 --> 00:01:29,883
For a subnet team, this is a place to develop the application around its idea while keeping the implementation visible to the people reviewing it.

15
00:01:35,150 --> 00:01:37,967
SubCreate focuses that workflow on subnet design.

16
00:01:38,117 --> 00:01:43,053
Describe the task and provide the dataset, then generate a structured recipe.

17
00:01:43,203 --> 00:01:53,225
The recipe brings the important decisions into view: the work a miner performs, the ground truth, the scoring components and the checks against shortcuts.

18
00:01:53,375 --> 00:01:59,582
Inspect the JSON, then use project assembly and code checks to move toward an implementation.

19
00:01:59,732 --> 00:02:07,211
This makes the evaluation problem something the team can discuss alongside the code, before relying on its incentives.

20
00:02:10,150 --> 00:02:15,433
Nodes connects the project to native miner and validator processes on the hosted runner.

21
00:02:15,583 --> 00:02:20,479
Use the process view to inspect status, read logs and manage its lifecycle.

22
00:02:20,629 --> 00:02:28,629
Runner also provides authenticated command execution with streamed output, making a simple Python command an immediate way to check the connection.

23
00:02:28,779 --> 00:02:32,898
These tools bring execution into the same workflow as the editor.

24
00:02:33,048 --> 00:02:40,272
A process has a concrete state and output that we can inspect while working through the next development step.

25
00:02:45,150 --> 00:02:49,328
The harness makes the scoring design something we can examine.

26
00:02:49,478 --> 00:02:55,388
This saved report compares honest, incorrect, slow and empty answers using controlled offline fixtures.

27
00:02:55,538 --> 00:03:01,448
Here, identical classification labels receive equal scores, because matching labels do not prove cheating.

28
00:03:01,598 --> 00:03:04,478
Incorrect and timed out responses score zero.

29
00:03:04,628 --> 00:03:14,000
These are real scoring calculations with simulated timing, giving the team a specific result to investigate before evaluating miners on a network.

30
00:03:15,150 --> 00:03:19,034
Return to TensorScan to explore observed blocks, calls and events.

31
00:03:19,184 --> 00:03:25,490
Switch the activity view, then open an underlying block to understand where the chart comes from.

32
00:03:25,640 --> 00:03:30,331
In the dashboard, choose a subnet and inspect its available neuron information.

33
00:03:30,481 --> 00:03:34,365
Account views add another way to follow a public address.

34
00:03:34,515 --> 00:03:42,434
Together, these views connect building with observation: we can ask what the chain actually shows and inspect the supporting details.

35
00:03:42,584 --> 00:03:49,293
The recent browser index is bounded, and missing observations remain visible as gaps in the available data.

36
00:03:55,150 --> 00:03:58,925
Wallet adds encrypted browser-local wallet control to the workspace.

37
00:03:59,075 --> 00:04:04,159
Here we inspect the interface without displaying recovery material or sending funds.

38
00:04:04,309 --> 00:04:13,755
Playground explains development funding paths for the selected network, while the Quest learning link offers a next step for understanding the ecosystem.

39
00:04:13,905 --> 00:04:22,042
These supporting tools help a builder move between learning, configuration and development without losing the context of the project.

40
00:04:25,150 --> 00:04:30,231
TensorStudio brings ideas, editable code, execution, offline evaluation and chain observation together.

41
00:04:30,381 --> 00:04:37,641
That is its practical value to Bittensor builders: a clearer loop for testing decisions and reviewing evidence.

42
00:04:37,791 --> 00:04:42,000
This free demonstration can sleep, and runner files are ephemeral.

43
00:04:42,150 --> 00:04:44,615
No development chain is deployed here.

44
00:04:44,765 --> 00:04:53,769
Running a process is separate from chain registration, endpoint serving, weight reveals and rewards; offline scores do not prove those milestones.

45
00:04:53,919 --> 00:04:59,000
Explore the deployed workspace, inspect the results, and keep improving the idea.
